const PATTERN_ACADEMY_3 = {
  "stack": {
    title: "Pattern 10 \u2014 Stack",
    sections: [
      { type: "text", heading: "1. What is a Stack?",
        body: "Stack follows Last In, First Out (LIFO). Operations: push(x) adds, pop() removes the top, peek() looks at the top. All are generally O(1)." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "The mental model",
        body: "     +-----+\n     |  C  |  <- last added (top)\n     +-----+\n     |  B  |\n     +-----+\n     |  A  |  <- first added\n     +-----+\n\nIf you remove something, you remove C first." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "2. Why does Stack exist?", tone: "gold",
        body: "The key question: do I need to process the most recently added unresolved item first? Consider three unresolved opening parentheses in a row. When you finally see a closing parenthesis, which opening one should it match? The most recent unmatched one. That is exactly LIFO.",
        conclusion: "Nested structure, then most recent unresolved item, then Stack." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "3. The most important Stack mental model", tone: "gold",
        body: "Do not memorize the shortcut 'parentheses means Stack.' Instead: a Stack stores things that are waiting for something to happen later. An opening bracket is waiting for its closing bracket. A previous state is waiting in case we need to go back to it. A nested operation is waiting for its inner operation to finish first.",
        conclusion: "This mental model helps you recognize brand-new problems, not just memorized ones." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4. Pattern A \u2014 Matching / Nested Structures (Valid Parentheses)",
        body: "input: { [ ( ) ] }\n\nsee {  -> push -> stack: { }\nsee [  -> push -> stack: {, [ }\nsee (  -> push -> stack: {, [, ( }\nsee )  -> must match top '(' -> pop -> stack: {, [ }\nsee ]  -> must match top '[' -> pop -> stack: { }\nsee }  -> must match top '{' -> pop -> stack: empty, valid\n\nIf a closing bracket does not match the top, the string is invalid." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "5. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "When you see any of these, immediately consider Stack:",
        items: ["Parentheses", "Brackets", "Nested", "Matching", "Balanced", "Expression"] },
      { type: "text", heading: "6. Pattern B \u2014 Expression evaluation",
        body: "Consider 2 + 3 * 4. Expressions contain operators, operands, nested parentheses, and precedence. A Stack can hold both numbers and operators as you scan the expression. Examples: Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation, Basic Calculator, Basic Calculator II. Mental model: given the current expression, I need to remember previous operations, so I use a Stack." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "7. Reverse Polish Notation \u2014 worked example",
        body: "2 1 + 3 *   means   (2 + 1) * 3\n\n2 -> push        stack: [2]\n1 -> push        stack: [2, 1]\n+ -> pop 1, pop 2, push 3   stack: [3]\n3 -> push        stack: [3, 3]\n* -> pop 3, pop 3, push 9   stack: [9]\n\nResult: 9\n\nThe most recently available operands are processed first, which is Stack behavior." },
      { type: "text", heading: "8. Pattern C \u2014 Nested Structures (Decode String)",
        body: "3[a2[c]] requires remembering the numbers 3 and 2 and the letter a while processing deeper nested content. When the inner bracket closes, you return to the previous context \u2014 this naturally leads to a Stack of previous states. Examples: Decode String, nested expressions, nested lists, file paths, XML/JSON-style parsing." },
      { type: "text", heading: "9. Stack as history",
        body: "Another mental model: a Stack stores history that you may need to return to. Browser navigation: Page A, then Page B, then Page C. Pressing back goes from C to B, then back again goes from B to A. This is conceptually a stack. Interview examples: undo, backtracking state, previous state, navigation history." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. Pattern D \u2014 Remove Previous Element",
        body: "characters = \"ab#c\"   where # means delete the previous character\n\na  -> [a]\nb  -> [a, b]\n#  -> [a]\nc  -> [a, c]\n\nResult: \"ac\"\n\n# is asking to remove the most recently added character, which is LIFO." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "11. Stack vs Queue", items: [
        { label: "Stack (LIFO)", body: "Use when the most recent unresolved item matters most. Example: nested parentheses \u2014 the most recent opening bracket gets matched first." },
        { label: "Queue (FIFO)", body: "Use when the oldest item should be processed first. Example: BFS \u2014 whatever was discovered first gets processed first." }
      ], conclusion: null },
      { type: "callout", heading: "12. Stack vs Recursion", tone: "gold",
        body: "A recursive DFS call chain A calls B calls C internally stores the call stack as C on top, then B, then A. Recursion is effectively using a stack of execution states, so you can often replace recursion with an explicit Stack.",
        conclusion: "This becomes useful when tree depth is huge, recursion may overflow, or you need an iterative traversal." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "13. Stack vs Monotonic Stack", items: [
        { label: "Normal Stack", body: "Question: what is the most recent unresolved item? Example: Valid Parentheses. Property: LIFO only." },
        { label: "Monotonic Stack", body: "Question: what is the nearest greater or smaller item? Example: Next Greater Element. Property: LIFO plus an ordering property \u2014 the stack remains ordered." }
      ], conclusion: "This distinction is extremely important \u2014 we will deep dive into Monotonic Stack next." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. Common Stack patterns (core vocabulary)",
        body: "Problem property              Stack usage\n-----------------------------  -----------\nMatching brackets              Stack\nNested structure                Stack\nExpression evaluation           Stack\nReverse Polish notation         Stack\nUndo / history                  Stack\nRemove most recent               Stack\nPrevious unresolved state        Stack\nIterative DFS                    Stack\nDecode nested string             Stack" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "15. Complexity",
        body: "push -> O(1)\npop  -> O(1)\npeek -> O(1)\n\nProcessing an entire array or string once:\nTime:  O(N)\nSpace: O(N)\n\nUsually each element is pushed and popped a constant number of times." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "16. The recognition decision tree",
        body: "Need to remember previous items?\n         |\n        YES\n         |\nDoes the MOST RECENT item need\nto be processed first?\n         |\n        YES\n         |\n       STACK\n\nThen ask: does the stack also need to remain\nin increasing or decreasing order?\n         |\n        YES\n         |\n  MONOTONIC STACK" },
      { type: "practice", heading: "17. Stack interview checklist",
        intro: "When you see a new problem, ask yourself these five questions:",
        items: [
          "Is there nesting? If yes, Stack is a candidate.",
          "Do I need the most recent unresolved element? If yes, Stack is a candidate.",
          "Am I evaluating an expression? If yes, Stack is a candidate.",
          "Do I need to undo or remove the latest operation? If yes, Stack is a candidate.",
          "Am I looking for the next or previous greater/smaller element? If yes, that is Monotonic Stack, not a normal stack."
        ],
        note: "Stack is now covered at the core interview level. We do not need to memorize implementations yet \u2014 the important thing is understanding why Stack is the right structure." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "18. What you should remember", dark: true,
        body: "                 STACK\n                   |\n         \"Most recent unresolved?\"\n                   |\n                  YES\n                   |\n       +-----------+-----------+\n       |           |           |\n    Matching     Nested     Expression\n    brackets    structures  evaluation\n       |           |           |\n       +-----------+-----------+\n                   |\n              Normal Stack\n\nAnd:\n\nNext Greater / Smaller?\n        |\nMonotonic Stack" },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Monotonic Stack",
        points: ["One of the highest-ROI patterns in coding interviews", "Very different from a normal Stack \u2014 maintains an ordering property"] }
    ]
  },

  "monotonic-stack": {
    title: "Pattern 11 \u2014 Monotonic Stack \u2b50\u2b50\u2b50\u2b50\u2b50",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Maintain a stack whose values are always increasing or decreasing. Example: [2, 1, 5, 3, 4] \u2014 find the next greater element for each position. Instead of repeatedly scanning forward, maintain unresolved elements in a stack.",
        conclusion: null },
      { type: "chips", heading: "Recognition triggers (extremely strong signals)",
        intro: "Immediately consider Monotonic Stack when you see:",
        items: ["Next greater", "Next smaller", "Previous greater", "Previous smaller", "Nearest greater", "Nearest smaller"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "Worked example \u2014 [2, 1, 5]",
        body: "start: stack = [2]\n\nsee 1: 1 < 2 -> keep, stack = [2, 1]\n\nsee 5: 5 > 1 -> 1's next greater = 5\n       5 > 2 -> 2's next greater = 5\n\nOne new element can resolve multiple previous elements efficiently." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "Increasing vs Decreasing stack", items: [
        { label: "Monotonically Increasing (e.g. 1, 2, 4, 7)", body: "Useful for Next smaller / related boundary problems." },
        { label: "Monotonically Decreasing (e.g. 9, 6, 4, 2)", body: "Useful for Next greater / maximum-related problems." }
      ], conclusion: "Don't blindly memorize which one \u2014 derive it from the condition you're solving." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "Important problems to eventually master",
        items: ["Next Greater Element", "Daily Temperatures", "Stock Span", "Largest Rectangle in Histogram", "Trapping Rain Water variants"] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Monotonic Queue / Deque",
        points: ["Same monotonic idea, but maintained over a deque instead of a stack", "Classic problem: Sliding Window Maximum in O(N)"] }
    ]
  },

  "monotonic-queue": {
    title: "Pattern 12 \u2014 Monotonic Queue / Deque",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The key idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Maintain a deque in monotonic order so the maximum or minimum of the current window is always available at the front. This is a smaller pattern than Stack, HashMap, or Sliding Window, but it is very useful for certain hard interview problems.",
        conclusion: "The classic problem is Sliding Window Maximum." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. First: what is a Deque?",
        body: "Deque means Double Ended Queue. You can add or remove from both ends.\n\n   front              back\n     |                  |\n  +-----+-----+-----+-----+\n  |  A  |  B  |  C  |  D  |\n  +-----+-----+-----+-----+\n\nOperations:\naddFirst()     addLast()\nremoveFirst()  removeLast()\npeekFirst()    peekLast()\n\nThis gives more flexibility than a normal queue." },
      { type: "text", heading: "2. Why do we need a Monotonic Queue?",
        body: "nums = [1,3,-1,-3,5,3,6,7], k = 3. We need the maximum of every window of size 3. Brute force scans all K elements for every window, giving O(N times K). With N around one million and K around five hundred thousand, that is far too slow." },
      { type: "text", heading: "3. Could we use a heap?",
        body: "Yes. A max heap can keep track of the largest element, giving approximately O(N log K), which is better than brute force. But we can do even better: O(N) with a Monotonic Deque." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "4. The core insight", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose our current window is [1, 3, -1]. Who can possibly become the maximum in the future? 1 is already smaller than 3, and 1 is older than 3. So once 3 arrives, 1 is useless as a future maximum candidate, and we can throw it away.",
        conclusion: null },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. Maintain decreasing order (for a maximum window)",
        body: "We maintain the deque in decreasing order.\n\nExample: 3, 1\n\nThe largest value is always at the front:\n\nfront\n  |\n[3, 1]\n\nTherefore deque.front() is the maximum." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6. Example step-by-step",
        body: "Array: [1, 3, -1, -3, 5]   Window size K = 3\n\nAdd 1:  deque = [1]\n\nAdd 3:  3 is greater than 1, so remove 1 (1 can never beat 3 while both are relevant)\n        deque = [3]\n\nAdd -1: deque = [3, -1]\n        front = 3, so the maximum is 3" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "7. What happens when an element leaves the window?", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose window = [1, 3, -1], then the window moves to [3, -1, -3] and the 1 leaves. We need to know: is the element leaving currently at the front of the deque? If yes, call removeFirst().",
        conclusion: "That's why we usually store indices, not just values." },
      { type: "text", heading: "8. Why store indices?",
        body: "nums = [1, 3, -1]. The deque stores index values, such as 1's index, then 3's index, and so on. Now we can check whether an index is less than the left boundary and remove expired elements. The deque stores candidate indices, not just values." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "9. The generic algorithm (mental model first, Java later)", dark: true,
        body: "for each right:\n\n    while deque not empty\n          AND deque.front is outside window:\n        removeFirst()\n\n    while deque not empty\n          AND nums[deque.back] <= nums[right]:\n        removeLast()\n\n    add right\n\n    if window size == K:\n        answer = nums[deque.front]" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. Why remove from the back?",
        body: "deque contains: [8, 6, 4]   new value: 7\n\n7 is greater than 4, so 4 can never be the maximum while 7 is available, remove it -> [8, 6]\n7 is greater than 6, so 6 also becomes useless, remove it -> [8]\nadd 7 -> [8, 7]\n\nThe deque remains decreasing." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "11. Why is it O(N)?", tone: "gold",
        body: "You may see a for loop containing a while loop and think O(N squared). But every index enters the deque exactly once and leaves the deque exactly once, so total operations equal O(N).",
        conclusion: "Time: O(N). Space: O(K)." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "12. Maximum vs Minimum", items: [
        { label: "Maximum window", body: "Deque decreasing, largest at front. Example: [9,7,5,2] gives a max of 9 at the front." },
        { label: "Minimum window", body: "Deque increasing, smallest at front. Example: [2,5,7,9] gives a min of 2 at the front." }
      ], conclusion: "Maximum uses a decreasing deque. Minimum uses an increasing deque." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "13. Monotonic Stack vs Monotonic Queue", items: [
        { label: "Monotonic Stack", body: "Usually asks: find the next or previous greater/smaller element. Structure: Stack. Example: Next Greater Element." },
        { label: "Monotonic Queue", body: "Usually asks: maintain min/max inside a moving window. Structure: Deque. Example: Sliding Window Maximum." }
      ], conclusion: null },
      { type: "chips", heading: "14. Recognition trigger",
        intro: "When you see sliding window plus maximum/minimum, think Monotonic Deque, especially when:",
        items: ["N is large", "K is large", "O(N times K) is too slow"] },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "15. But a heap can also solve it \u2014 know the trade-off", items: [
        { label: "Brute force", body: "O(N times K)" },
        { label: "Heap", body: "O(N log K)" },
        { label: "Monotonic Deque", body: "O(N)" }
      ], conclusion: "If the interviewer asks whether you can think of another solution, discussing this trade-off is strong interview behavior." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "16. The general mental model", tone: "gold",
        body: "The most important sentence: keep only candidates that can still become the answer. For maximum: if a new value is greater than smaller candidates, discard those smaller candidates. For minimum: if a new value is smaller than larger candidates, discard those larger candidates.",
        conclusion: "This is exactly the same philosophy as Monotonic Stack: remove candidates that can no longer win." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "17. Tier 2 progress",
        items: ["10. Stack", "11. Monotonic Stack", "12. Monotonic Queue",
          { label: "13. Heap", done: false }, { label: "14. Two Heaps", done: false },
          { label: "15. Fast/Slow Pointers", done: false }, { label: "16. Linked List Reversal", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Heap / Priority Queue",
        points: ["A major pattern that appears very frequently in Google/Uber/Amazon interviews", "Min Heap vs Max Heap, Top K, Kth largest/smallest, K closest, scheduling, merge K sorted lists, streaming problems", "Why Heap instead of Sorting, why Heap instead of HashMap, O(N log K) reasoning, and the recognition decision tree"] }
    ]
  },

  "heap-priority-queue": {
    title: "Pattern 13 — Heap / Priority Queue ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The key idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "When you repeatedly need the smallest or largest element among a changing set of candidates, use a Heap. This is a major interview pattern that appears in Google, Uber, Amazon, and many other product-company interviews.",
        conclusion: null },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "1. What is a Heap? Min Heap vs Max Heap", items: [
        { label: "Min Heap", body: "Smallest element at the top. Use when you need the smallest candidate next." },
        { label: "Max Heap", body: "Largest element at the top. Use when you need the largest candidate next." }
      ], conclusion: "Unlike sorting, the entire collection doesn't need to be ordered. You only care about who is the current best candidate." },
      { type: "text", heading: "2. Why does Heap exist?",
        body: "Given [8, 3, 10, 2, 7, 5], repeatedly asking for the smallest number means sorting costs O(N log N) every time. If new elements keep arriving, you don't want to repeatedly re-sort everything. A Heap gives insert O(log N), remove min/max O(log N), and peek min/max O(1) — ideal when you're repeatedly asking who is the next smallest/largest." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. Heap mental model",
        body: "             highest priority\n                    |\n                  [2]\n                /     \\\n              [4]     [5]\n             /   \\\n           [8]   [7]\n\nYou don't care about complete sorting — you only guarantee the minimum is at the top (for a Min Heap)." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "5. Recognition Trigger #1 — Top K ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐", tone: "gold",
        body: "Find the K largest elements. Example: [3,2,1,5,6,4], K=2 -> answer [6,5]. You could sort in O(N log N), but if K is small relative to N, use a Min Heap of size K instead.",
        conclusion: "Why Min Heap? Because we want to keep the best K elements, and the smallest among those K should be the easiest one to remove." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6. Top K mental model",
        body: "K = 3, process: 10, 7, 8, 5, 12\n\nMaintain a Min Heap.\n\nAfter processing 10, 7, 8, 5 (keeping best 3): [7, 8, 10]\n\nThen 12 arrives: 12 > 7 (the smallest), so remove 7\nResult: [8, 10, 12]\n\nWe keep only the best 3. Complexity: O(N log K)." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "7. Why Min Heap for K largest? (this confuses people)", tone: "gold",
        body: "Find K largest: we maintain K largest candidates, and we want to remove the smallest among them — therefore Min Heap. Find K smallest: we maintain K smallest candidates, and we want to remove the largest among them — therefore Max Heap.",
        conclusion: "K largest -> Min Heap of size K. K smallest -> Max Heap of size K." },
      { type: "text", heading: "8. Recognition Trigger #2 — Kth Largest / Smallest",
        body: "Find the Kth largest element using a Min Heap of size K. At the end, heap.peek() is the Kth largest. Example: [3,2,1,5,6,4], K=2 -> keep [5,6] -> the smallest of these is 5 -> the 2nd largest is 5." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "9. Heap vs Sorting", items: [
        { label: "Sorting", body: "O(N log N). Needed when you require the entire dataset fully ordered." },
        { label: "Heap", body: "O(N log K). Needed when you only need the top K, or repeated min/max while data changes." }
      ], conclusion: "With N = 1,000,000 and K = 10, log(10) is far smaller than log(1,000,000) — Heap is much more appropriate. The interview question is: do I need the entire data sorted, or only the top K?" },
      { type: "text", heading: "10. Recognition Trigger #3 — K Closest",
        body: "Find K points closest to the origin, using distance-squared = x squared + y squared. We need the K smallest distances, so maintain a Max Heap of size K — because among our K closest candidates, we want to remove the farthest when a better point arrives. K closest -> Max Heap. Same principle: keep K winners, and the heap stores the easiest winner to kick out." },
      { type: "text", heading: "11. Recognition Trigger #4 — Scheduling",
        body: "Tasks with different finish times: if you repeatedly need to know which task finishes earliest, use a Min Heap storing finish times. This appears in Meeting Rooms II, CPU scheduling, task scheduling, event simulation, and server allocation." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "12. Meeting Rooms II — Heap + Intervals",
        body: "Meetings sorted by start: [0,30] [5,10] [15,20]\n\nMin Heap of ending times: [30]\n\nNext meeting [5,10]: earliest room ends at 30, can't reuse it -> add 10 -> heap: [10, 30]\n\nNext meeting [15,20]: earliest ending room is 10. Since 10 <= 15, reuse it.\n\nSo: Heap + Intervals." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. Recognition Trigger #5 — Merge K Sorted Lists",
        body: "List A: 1 -> 4 -> 7\nList B: 2 -> 5 -> 8\nList C: 3 -> 6 -> 9\n\nAt any moment we only care about the smallest current element from each list.\nPut current heads into a Min Heap: [1, 2, 3] -> take 1\nInsert next from A: [2, 3, 4] -> take 2\nContinue...\n\nComplexity: O(N log K), where K = number of lists." },
      { type: "text", heading: "14. Recognition Trigger #6 — Streaming Data",
        body: "Values arrive continuously (5, 2, 10, 3, 7, ...) and after each insertion you need the current smallest/largest/top K. Sorting every time is expensive — a Heap can maintain the answer dynamically. This is why heaps are useful for streaming problems." },
      { type: "text", heading: "15. Heap + HashMap",
        body: "Example: Top K frequent elements. First build a HashMap of value -> frequency, then use a Heap to select the top K frequencies. Frequency problem + Top K leads to HashMap + Heap — an extremely common combination." },
      { type: "text", heading: "16. Heap + Graph",
        body: "Heaps also appear in graph algorithms. In Dijkstra, at every step you process the node with the smallest current distance — Graph + Min Heap. This becomes important when we reach Graph patterns." },
      { type: "text", heading: "17. Heap + Greedy",
        body: "Heaps often implement greedy decisions, such as always choosing the smallest available task or always choosing the largest available reward. Greedy choice + need efficient min/max selection leads to Heap." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "18. The most important Heap pattern", tone: "gold",
        body: "Forget individual problems. Remember: I have many candidates, but at every step I only care about the best one.",
        conclusion: "Need smallest -> Min Heap. Need largest -> Max Heap. K largest -> Min Heap size K. K smallest -> Max Heap size K." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "19. Heap decision tree",
        body: "                 HEAP?\n                   |\n          Need repeated min/max?\n                   |\n                  YES\n                   |\n       +-----------+------------+\n       |           |            |\n    Top K       Scheduling    Streaming\n       |           |            |\n  Heap size K   Min Heap     Dynamic Heap\n\nAnother branch:\n\nNeed K largest?  -> Min Heap K\nNeed K smallest? -> Max Heap K" },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "20. Heap vs Sorting — the interview follow-up", items: [
        { label: "Need complete ordering?", body: "Sorting, O(N log N)." },
        { label: "Need only top K?", body: "Heap, O(N log K)." },
        { label: "Need repeated min/max while data changes?", body: "Heap." },
        { label: "Need all elements ordered?", body: "Heap usually isn't enough — sorting may be simpler." }
      ], conclusion: null },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "21. Heap vs Monotonic Deque", items: [
        { label: "Heap", body: "O(N log K). Works for Sliding Window Maximum." },
        { label: "Monotonic Deque", body: "O(N). Optimal for Sliding Window Maximum." }
      ], conclusion: "If the interviewer asks 'can you optimize?', moving from Heap to Monotonic Deque is a great example of pattern progression." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "22. Java", dark: true,
        body: "Min Heap:\nPriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();\n\nMax Heap:\nPriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap =\n    new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());\n\nCustom objects:\nPriorityQueue<Node> pq =\n    new PriorityQueue<>(\n        Comparator.comparingInt(n -> n.distance)\n    );\n\nDon't focus on memorizing syntax now — the important part is: why is this the correct data structure?" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "23. Complexity",
        body: "peek   O(1)\noffer  O(log N)\npoll   O(log N)\n\nFor a heap of size K: O(log K)\n\nTherefore Top K: O(N log K)\nSpace: O(K)" },
      { type: "chips", heading: "24. Recognition triggers",
        intro: "When you see any of these, think Heap, then ask: Min Heap or Max Heap?",
        items: ["Top K", "Kth largest", "Kth smallest", "K closest", "Repeated minimum", "Repeated maximum", "Smallest next", "Largest next", "Scheduling", "Merge K sorted", "Streaming min/max"] },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "25. Tier 2 progress",
        items: ["10. Stack", "11. Monotonic Stack", "12. Monotonic Queue", "13. Heap / Priority Queue",
          { label: "14. Two Heaps", done: false }, { label: "15. Fast & Slow Pointers", done: false },
          { label: "16. Linked List Reversal", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Two Heaps",
        points: ["A smaller but important pattern", "Central idea: split the data into two halves and maintain each half with a different Heap", "Classic application: Median from Data Stream"] }
    ]
  },

  "two-heaps": {
    title: "Pattern 14 — Two Heaps ⭐⭐⭐⭐",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Split the data into two groups and use one heap to manage each group. This is a specialized Heap pattern \u2014 you don't need dozens of problems for it, but you should recognize the structure immediately when it appears.",
        conclusion: "The classic example is Median of a Data Stream." },
      { type: "text", heading: "1. The problem",
        body: "Numbers arrive one at a time (5, 10, 3, 8, 7, ...) and after every insertion we want the median. If we sort after every insertion (insert, sort, find median), that's expensive. We need to maintain the data dynamically." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Split the data into two halves",
        body: "[1, 3, 5, 8, 10, 12]\n\nSmaller half       Larger half\n[1, 3, 5]          [8, 10, 12]\n\nFrom the smaller half we need the largest element.\nFrom the larger half we need the smallest element.\n\nTherefore:\nSmaller half -> Max Heap\nLarger half  -> Min Heap\n\nThis is the Two Heap pattern." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. Why these two heaps?",
        body: "             MEDIAN\n               |\n        +--------------+\n        |              |\n   smaller half    larger half\n        |              |\n    Max Heap        Min Heap\n        |              |\n     largest         smallest\n\nThe two values closest to the boundary give us the median.\n\nFor [1,3,5] [8,10,12]:\nmaxHeap.peek() = 5\nminHeap.peek() = 8\nMedian = (5 + 8) / 2 = 6.5" },
      { type: "variants", heading: "4. The three invariants (the most important part)", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Ordering", question: "Every value in the smaller half must be <= every value in the larger half.",
          diagram: "small half | large half\n-----------|-----------\n1 3 5      | 8 10 12\n           ^\n        boundary" },
        { id: "2", title: "Balance", question: "The heap sizes should differ by at most 1.",
          note: "OK: small=3, large=3 or small=4, large=3. NOT OK: small=7, large=1." },
        { id: "3", title: "Boundary Elements", question: "The only elements we need to find the median are maxHeap.peek() and minHeap.peek().",
          note: "That's why we don't need the entire data sorted." }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "5. Example step-by-step",
        body: "Add 5:  small=[5]        large=[]        median = 5\n\nAdd 10: 10 > 5, goes to Min Heap\n        small=[5]        large=[10]      median = (5+10)/2 = 7.5\n\nAdd 3:  3 belongs in smaller half\n        small=[5,3]      large=[10]      sizes 2 vs 1, median = 5\n\nAdd 8:  8 belongs in larger half\n        small=[5,3]      large=[8,10]    median = (5+8)/2 = 6.5" },
      { type: "text", heading: "6. What if the heaps become unbalanced?",
        body: "Suppose small=4, large=2 \u2014 the difference is greater than 1. Move the largest element from the smaller half (small.poll()) into the larger half (large.offer(...)). Now small=3, large=3, balanced again. Similarly, if large=4 and small=2, move large.poll() to small." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "7. The generic algorithm", dark: true,
        body: "add(x)\n\nif small is empty OR x <= small.max:\n    add x to small\nelse:\n    add x to large\n\nbalance the heaps\n\nmedian = based on their tops" },
      { type: "chips", heading: "8. Recognition trigger",
        intro: "Two Heaps should come to mind when you see the word 'median', especially:",
        items: ["Median from Data Stream", "Running median", "Online median", "Dynamic median"] },
      { type: "text", heading: "9. Other applications",
        body: "Beyond median: dynamic median (values continuously arrive, maintain median), balancing two groups (lower half / upper half), running percentile-like problems (some variations use two priority queues to maintain a boundary between groups), and scheduling/resource balancing (maintaining available candidates + active candidates, though not every such problem requires Two Heaps)." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "10. Two Heaps vs Sorting", items: [
        { label: "Sorting each time", body: "Expensive: insert, sort, insert, sort, insert, sort, repeated for every new value." },
        { label: "Two Heaps", body: "Maintain the structure incrementally: insert is O(log N), median is O(1)." }
      ], conclusion: "Two Heaps are especially useful for online / streaming data." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "11. Two Heaps vs One Heap", items: [
        { label: "One Heap", body: "If you only need the smallest/largest, use a Min Heap or Max Heap." },
        { label: "Two Heaps", body: "If you need a boundary separating two groups, use Max Heap + Min Heap. For median: smaller half -> Max Heap, larger half -> Min Heap." }
      ], conclusion: null },
      { type: "callout", heading: "12. The mental model", tone: "gold",
        body: "Don't memorize 'median means two heaps.' Instead: need to maintain a boundary between two groups, so split data into two halves, then you need the max of the lower half plus the min of the upper half, which leads to Two Heaps.",
        conclusion: "This is the transferable idea." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. Complexity",
        body: "For N inserted elements:\nInsertion: O(log N)\nMedian:    O(1)\nSpace:     O(N)\n\nIf the heaps contain approximately N/2 elements each, log(N/2) is approximately equal to log N." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. Java structure", dark: true,
        body: "PriorityQueue<Integer> lower =\n    new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());\n\nPriorityQueue<Integer> upper =\n    new PriorityQueue<>();\n\nSo: lower -> Max Heap, upper -> Min Heap\n\nThen maintain:\nlower.size() == upper.size()\nOR\nabs(lower.size() - upper.size()) == 1\n\nUnderstand the invariant before memorizing the code." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "15. Two Heaps recognition cheat sheet",
        body: "Problem property                    Pattern\n-----------------------------------  ---------\nRunning median                       Two Heaps\nMedian from stream                   Two Heaps\nDynamic median                       Two Heaps\nSplit into lower/upper half          Two Heaps\nNeed max of lower + min of upper     Two Heaps\nRepeated single min/max              One Heap\nTop K                                Heap\nKth largest                          Heap\nK closest                            Heap" },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 2 progress",
        items: ["10. Stack", "11. Monotonic Stack", "12. Monotonic Queue / Deque", "13. Heap / Priority Queue", "14. Two Heaps",
          { label: "15. Fast & Slow Pointers", done: false }, { label: "16. Linked List Reversal", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Fast & Slow Pointers",
        points: ["You'll see that Two Pointers is actually a family of techniques, not just the left/right approach", "Cycle detection, finding the middle, linked-list palindrome, finding cycle entry, Happy Number", "Why slow and fast must eventually meet, and when Fast/Slow works on arrays as well as linked lists"] }
    ]
  },

  "fast-slow-pointer": {
    title: "Pattern 15 — Fast & Slow Pointers ⭐⭐⭐⭐",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is a specialized Two Pointer pattern: use two pointers moving at different speeds to detect cycles, find a midpoint, or detect relative movement in a sequence.",
        conclusion: "The classic setup: slow moves 1 step, fast moves 2 steps." },
      { type: "text", heading: "1. Why does this pattern exist?",
        body: "Imagine two runners on a circular track. One runs 1 step per turn, the other runs 2 steps per turn. If the track is circular, eventually the faster runner catches the slower runner. That's the intuition behind cycle detection." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Classic problem — Linked List Cycle",
        body: "1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4\n        ^     |\n        +-----+\n\nThere is a cycle: 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 2\n\nA HashSet<Node> approach works but requires O(N) space.\nFast/Slow gives O(N) time and O(1) space." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. The algorithm",
        body: "Start:\nslow = head\nfast = head\n\nMove:\nslow = slow.next\nfast = fast.next.next\n\nIf there is a cycle: slow == fast eventually.\nIf fast reaches null: no cycle." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "4. Why do they meet? (worth understanding, not memorizing)", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose both pointers are inside a cycle. Every iteration, slow moves 1 and fast moves 2. So relative to slow, fast gains 1 position per iteration. Since the cycle has finite length, eventually the faster pointer catches the slower pointer.",
        conclusion: "That's the mathematical reason they must meet." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "5. Recognition Trigger #1",
        intro: "When you see this question, immediately think Fast & Slow:",
        items: ["Does this linked list contain a cycle?"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6. Pattern #2 — Find Middle of Linked List",
        body: "1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5\n\nStart:   slow = 1, fast = 1\nMove:    slow -> 2, fast -> 3\nAgain:   slow -> 3, fast -> 5\n\nNow fast reaches the end, so slow = middle (which is 3).\n\nReason: fast travels twice as quickly, so when fast finishes, slow has traveled half the distance." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "7. Recognition Trigger #2",
        intro: "When you see this question, think Fast & Slow:",
        items: ["Find the middle of a linked list"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. Pattern #3 — Find Cycle Entry (Floyd's Algorithm)",
        body: "1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5\n        ^         |\n        +---------+\n\nAfter slow == fast, you know a cycle exists. But the question might ask:\nwhere does the cycle begin?\n\npointer1 = head\npointer2 = meeting point\n\nMove both one step at a time:\npointer1++\npointer2++\n\nThey meet at the Cycle Entry." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "9. Why does that work?", tone: "gold",
        body: "You don't need to memorize the mathematical proof initially. The useful interview intuition: head -> distance A -> cycle entry -> distance B -> meeting point. Because the fast pointer traveled twice as far, the distances line up such that moving one pointer from the head and one from the meeting point at the same speed causes them to meet exactly at the cycle entry.",
        conclusion: "We'll derive the full proof when you practice the problem." },
      { type: "text", heading: "10. Pattern #4 — Palindrome Linked List",
        body: "Problem: is 1 -> 2 -> 2 -> 1 a palindrome? We need to compare 1<->1 and 2<->2. A common O(1)-space approach: Step 1) find the middle using Fast + Slow. Step 2) reverse the second half. Step 3) compare the two halves." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "This combines two patterns", tone: "gold",
        body: "Fast/Slow (find middle) + Linked List Reversal (reverse second half). This is an important combination you'll see again.",
        conclusion: null },
      { type: "callout", heading: "11. Pattern #5 — Happy Number (where Fast/Slow gets interesting)", tone: "gold",
        body: "Determine whether repeatedly replacing a number with the sum of squares of its digits eventually reaches 1. Example: 19 -> 82 -> 68 -> 100 -> 1. But some numbers enter a cycle instead. Think of the transformation number -> next number -> next number as effectively a linked list: 19 -> 82 -> 68 -> 100 -> 1, or in general x -> y -> z -> x. If the sequence cycles, Fast + Slow can detect it.",
        conclusion: "Fast/Slow is not limited to linked-list nodes. It works whenever you have a deterministic sequence x -> next(x)." },
      { type: "text", heading: "12. Arrays can also have cycles",
        body: "Suppose nums[i] tells you the next index to jump to. If every element tells you where to jump next, you can conceptually create index -> next index and use Fast/Slow. This appears in some duplicate-number problems: find the duplicate number without modifying the array and using O(1) extra space. The array can be interpreted as a linked structure. This is an advanced but important application." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "13. Fast/Slow vs Normal Two Pointers", items: [
        { label: "Opposite-direction Two Pointers (L -> ... <- R)", body: "Used for sorted arrays, palindromes, pair problems." },
        { label: "Same-direction Two Pointers (slow ->, fast ->->)", body: "Used for cycle detection, finding the middle, read/write patterns, linked lists." }
      ], conclusion: "So Fast/Slow is a specialized Two Pointer family." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. Recognition decision tree",
        body: "Two pointers?\n      |\nDo they move at different speeds?\n      |\n     YES\n      |\n  Fast & Slow\n      |\n      +-- Looking for a cycle?      -> Cycle Detection\n      +-- Looking for midpoint?     -> Find Middle\n      +-- Deterministic next-state\n          sequence?                -> Cycle Detection" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "15. Complexity",
        body: "Linked list cycle:  Time O(N), Space O(1)\nFind middle:         Time O(N), Space O(1)\nCycle entry:         Time O(N), Space O(1)\n\nThis is the major advantage over HashSet-based solutions." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "16. Important interview follow-up", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose you say: 'I'll use a HashSet to detect the cycle.' The interviewer asks: 'Can you do it in O(1) extra space?' Your answer: Fast & Slow Pointers.",
        conclusion: "That's why understanding this pattern is valuable." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "17. The core pattern vocabulary", dark: true,
        body: "             FAST / SLOW\n                  |\n       +----------+----------+\n       |          |          |\n     Cycle      Middle    Palindrome\n       |\n       |\n   Cycle Entry\n\nAnd the broader idea:\n\nDeterministic sequence\n        |\nx -> next(x)\n        |\nCould it cycle?\n        |\nFast + Slow" },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 2 progress",
        items: ["10. Stack", "11. Monotonic Stack", "12. Monotonic Queue / Deque", "13. Heap / Priority Queue", "14. Two Heaps", "15. Fast & Slow Pointers",
          { label: "16. Linked List Reversal", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Linked List Reversal / Manipulation",
        points: ["The last Tier 2 pattern", "Reverse entire list, reverse a sublist, reverse K nodes, merge two sorted lists", "Reconnect nodes safely, dummy node technique, in-place pointer manipulation, how to avoid losing nodes", "After this, Tier 2 pattern vocabulary is complete, and we move to the much larger Trees + Graphs family"] }
    ]
  },

  "linked-list-reversal-manipulation": {
    title: "Pattern 16 — Linked List Reversal & Manipulation ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is the final pattern in Tier 2. Unlike HashMap or Heap, this isn't about choosing a sophisticated data structure \u2014 it's about mastering pointer movement.",
        conclusion: "Change links between nodes without losing access to the rest of the list." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Basic Linked List",
        body: "1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> null\n\nEach node contains:\nvalue\nnext\n\nConceptually:\nNode\n |-- value\n +-- next --> another Node" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "2. The biggest danger", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 and you want 2 -> 1. If you do current.next = previous before saving the original current.next, you've lost access to 3 -> 4.",
        conclusion: "Always save the next node before changing the current pointer. This is the fundamental rule." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. Basic Reversal",
        body: "Original: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> null\nTarget:   4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> null\n\nMaintain three pointers: previous, current, next\n\nInitially:\nprevious = null\ncurrent = 1" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4. One iteration",
        body: "Current state:\nprevious    current\n   |           |\n null         1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4\n\n1) next = current.next        (next -> 2)\n2) current.next = previous    (1 -> null)\n3) previous = current, current = next\n\nNow:\nprevious    current\n   |           |\n   1           2 -> 3 -> 4\n\nRepeat." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "5. The three-step mental model", tone: "gold",
        body: "Every iteration: 1) SAVE next. 2) REVERSE current.next. 3) MOVE pointers forward.",
        conclusion: "SAVE -> REVERSE -> MOVE. This is one template worth knowing almost mechanically." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6. Basic Complexity",
        body: "For N nodes:\nTime:  O(N)\nSpace: O(1)\n\nThat's why linked-list reversal is such a common interview question." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "7. Pattern A — Reverse Entire List",
        intro: "1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 becomes 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1. Recognition trigger \u2014 immediately think three pointers:",
        items: ["previous", "current", "next"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. Pattern B — Reverse Between Two Positions",
        body: "1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5\n\nReverse positions 2 through 4:\n1 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 5\n\nThree regions: Before | Reversed | After\n\n1 | 2 3 4 | 5\n      v\n1 | 4 3 2 | 5\n\nThe hard part isn't the reversal itself \u2014 it's reconnecting the reversed section to the unchanged parts." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "9. Dummy Node \u2b50", tone: "gold",
        body: "A very important linked-list technique. Suppose 1 -> 2 -> 3 and the operation might modify the head. Instead of handling head as a special case, create dummy -> 1 -> 2 -> 3. Now dummy.next is the actual list.",
        conclusion: "This simplifies: insert at head, delete nodes, reverse sublists, merge lists, remove Nth node, partition lists." },
      { type: "text", heading: "10. Why dummy nodes are useful",
        body: "Without a dummy: if removing head, handle a special case; else, handle the normal case. With a dummy: dummy -> head, and everything becomes previous.next = ... the same logic everywhere. Dummy node removes head-edge-case complexity." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "11. Pattern C — Reverse K Nodes",
        body: "1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> 6\n\nReverse every 3:\n3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 6 -> 5 -> 4\n\n[1 2 3] [4 5 6]\n    v        v\n[3 2 1] [6 5 4]\n\nThis combines: Linked List Reversal + Boundary detection + Reconnection.\nThe tricky part is knowing: do we have K nodes remaining?" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "12. Pattern D — Merge Two Sorted Lists",
        body: "A: 1 -> 4 -> 7\nB: 2 -> 3 -> 8\n\nUse two pointers: A -> 1, B -> 2. Choose smaller (1), move A.\nThen A -> 4, B -> 2. Choose smaller (2). Continue.\n\nResult: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 7 -> 8\n\nThis is: Two Pointers + Linked List Manipulation." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "13. Pattern E — Remove Nth Node From End",
        body: "1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5\n\nRemove 2nd from end: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 5\n\nHow to find it in one pass? Use fast and slow.\nFirst move fast ahead by N.\nThen move both fast and slow together.\nWhen fast reaches the end, slow is at the node before the one to remove.\n\nThis combines: Fast/Slow + Dummy Node. A very important interview combination." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. Pattern F — Partition Linked List",
        body: "Put nodes smaller than X before nodes greater than or equal to X.\n\n1 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 5 -> 2,  x = 3\n\nResult: 1 -> 2 -> 2 -> 4 -> 3 -> 5\n\nUse two lists: a 'less' list and a 'greater/equal' list, then connect them. Similar to array partitioning." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "15. Pattern G — Reorder List",
        body: "1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5\n\nReorder to: 1 -> 5 -> 2 -> 4 -> 3\n\nThis combines multiple patterns:\nFind middle (Fast/Slow)\n  -> Reverse second half (Linked List Reversal)\n  -> Merge two halves (Two Pointers)\n\nA great example of how interview problems are often combinations of patterns." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "16. Linked List Pattern Map", dark: true,
        body: "             LINKED LIST\n                  |\n       +----------+----------+\n       |          |          |\n   Fast/Slow   Reversal   Two Pointers\n       |          |          |\n     Cycle     Reverse     Merge\n     Middle    K-group     Compare\n     Nth-end   Sublist     Partition\n\nAnd:\n\nDummy Node\n    |\nRemove head edge cases\n    |\nSimplify pointer manipulation" },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "17. Recognition Cheat Sheet",
        body: "Trigger                Pattern\n----------------------  --------------------------\nReverse linked list     Reversal\nReverse K nodes         Reversal\nReverse sublist         Reversal\nFind middle             Fast/Slow\nDetect cycle            Fast/Slow\nFind cycle entry        Fast/Slow\nNth node from end       Fast/Slow\nPalindrome list         Fast/Slow + Reverse\nMerge sorted lists      Two Pointers\nRemove nodes            Dummy + pointers\nInsert nodes            Dummy + pointers\nPartition list          Two lists + pointers\nReorder list            Middle + Reverse + Merge" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "18. The most important Linked List rule", tone: "gold",
        body: "When manipulating pointers: never change a pointer before you've saved the node you still need.",
        conclusion: "SAVE -> CHANGE -> MOVE. If you remember only one thing from this pattern, remember that." },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 2 — COMPLETE! All 7 patterns done",
        items: ["10. Stack", "11. Monotonic Stack", "12. Monotonic Queue / Deque", "13. Heap / Priority Queue", "14. Two Heaps", "15. Fast & Slow Pointers", "16. Linked List Reversal / Manipulation"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "Your full pattern vocabulary so far (Tier 1 + Tier 2 = 16 patterns)", dark: true,
        body: "Tier 1 — Arrays / Strings\n1.  HashMap / HashSet\n2.  Two Pointers\n3.  Sliding Window\n4.  Prefix Sum\n5.  Binary Search\n6.  Sorting + Scanning\n7.  Intervals\n8.  Kadane / Running Optimization\n9.  Bit Manipulation\n\nTier 2 — Stack / Heap / Linked List\n10. Stack\n11. Monotonic Stack\n12. Monotonic Queue\n13. Heap\n14. Two Heaps\n15. Fast & Slow\n16. Linked List Manipulation" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "What comes next — Tier 3: Trees & Graphs", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is a big one for Google/Uber preparation: Tree DFS, Tree BFS, BST, LCA, Tree DP, Graph DFS, Graph BFS, Multi-Source BFS, Topological Sort, Union Find, Shortest Path, Dijkstra, 0-1 BFS, Bellman-Ford, Minimum Spanning Tree (17-31).",
        conclusion: "We'll learn these the same way: why the pattern exists -> mental model -> recognition triggers -> variations -> when NOT to use it -> combinations. After finishing the pattern map, we'll return to Tier 1 and start actual problem practice, so you're not trying to recognize patterns you haven't learned yet." },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Tree DFS",
        points: ["Start of the much larger Tree + Graph pattern family (Tier 3)", "Path problems, return-value DFS, and Tree DP"] }
    ]
  }
};
